1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:02,890
Singapore's Harbourmaster of 1604:

2
00:00:02,970 --> 00:00:06,900
Erédia's Map and the
Lost Portuguese-Era Port

3
00:00:06,900 --> 00:00:11,932
In 1604 the Malacca-born Portuguese
mapmaker Manuel Godinho de

4
00:00:12,012 --> 00:00:17,125
Erédia drew a small sketch map
of the waters around Singapore.

5
00:00:17,125 --> 00:00:21,362
Along the island's shore he wrote
a handful of Malay place names,

6
00:00:21,442 --> 00:00:24,417
among them Tanah Merah,
Bedok and Tanjong Rhu,

7
00:00:24,497 --> 00:00:28,402
which are still on the map
today, and one word which is not:

8
00:00:28,482 --> 00:00:32,600
Xabandaria, the quarters of the
shahbandar, the harbourmaster.

9
00:00:32,600 --> 00:00:37,270
Two hundred years after the fall of
the Singapura of the Malay Annals,

10
00:00:37,350 --> 00:00:39,781
and two hundred years before Raffles,

11
00:00:39,861 --> 00:00:44,000
the island still had a port,
and someone was in charge of it.

12
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:48,899
The sketch is titled, in Portuguese,
a "chorographic description

13
00:00:48,979 --> 00:00:53,725
of the straits of Singapore and
Sabam", and it is dated 1604.

14
00:00:53,725 --> 00:00:58,078
It is one of the maps in
Erédia's Description of Malacca,

15
00:00:58,158 --> 00:01:02,825
Southern India and Cathay, a
book he finished in about 1613.

16
00:01:02,825 --> 00:01:07,405
The original manuscript is in
the Royal Library in Brussels,

17
00:01:07,485 --> 00:01:13,000
and it became widely known after a
facsimile was printed there in 1882.

18
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:17,753
The map shows Singapore as an island
set in the mouth of the Johor River,

19
00:01:17,833 --> 00:01:21,275
with the two channels that
ships could take past it.

20
00:01:21,275 --> 00:01:25,870
The Old Strait ran north of the
island along today's Johor Strait,

21
00:01:25,950 --> 00:01:28,349
and the New Strait ran south of it,

22
00:01:28,429 --> 00:01:31,900
past Blakang Mati, the
island now called Sentosa.

23
00:01:31,900 --> 00:01:36,053
Along the east coast Erédia
wrote, in Portuguese spellings,

24
00:01:36,133 --> 00:01:39,712
the names of Tanjong Rusa
near Changi, Tanah Merah,

25
00:01:39,792 --> 00:01:44,025
Sungei Bedok and Tanjong Rhu,
and then the word Xabandaria.

26
00:01:44,025 --> 00:01:47,000
Its exact spot is uncertain.

27
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:50,788
The National Library reads it
as lying west of Tanjong Rhu,

28
00:01:50,868 --> 00:01:55,640
and the historian Peter Borschberg,
who has written most about these maps,

29
00:01:55,720 --> 00:01:59,312
places it somewhere between
Tanjong Rhu and Pulau Brani,

30
00:01:59,392 --> 00:02:03,850
the stretch of shore that takes in
the mouth of the Singapore River.

31
00:02:03,850 --> 00:02:06,689
Erédia was born in Malacca

32
00:02:06,769 --> 00:02:09,575
on the 16th of July 1563.

33
00:02:09,575 --> 00:02:14,161
His father, João de Erédia
Aquaviva, was a Portuguese official,

34
00:02:14,241 --> 00:02:19,567
and his mother, Dona Elena Vessiva,
was a Bugis noblewoman from Sulawesi,

35
00:02:19,647 --> 00:02:23,350
the daughter of a recently
baptised king of Suppa.

36
00:02:23,350 --> 00:02:26,687
He went to the Jesuit
school in Malacca and at

37
00:02:26,767 --> 00:02:29,733
13 was sent to the Jesuit college in Goa,

38
00:02:29,813 --> 00:02:33,825
where he learned mathematics,
astronomy and mapmaking.

39
00:02:33,825 --> 00:02:37,032
He spent most of his career in Portuguese

40
00:02:37,112 --> 00:02:40,800
India as a cosmographer,
engineer and soldier.

41
00:02:40,800 --> 00:02:46,032
In 1601 he was sent back to Malacca
to lead an expedition in search

42
00:02:46,112 --> 00:02:51,425
of "Meridional India", a land of
gold believed to lie to the south.

43
00:02:51,425 --> 00:02:56,075
The war around the Straits kept
him there for four years instead.

44
00:02:56,075 --> 00:02:59,770
Borschberg writes that in
1604 Erédia led a Portuguese

45
00:02:59,850 --> 00:03:04,105
squadron exploring Singapore,
the straits and the Johor River,

46
00:03:04,185 --> 00:03:07,880
which is when the sketch is
dated, though the National

47
00:03:07,960 --> 00:03:12,225
Library's account doubts that
he ever set foot on the island.

48
00:03:12,225 --> 00:03:15,945
He fell ill, went back
to Goa in about 1605,

49
00:03:16,025 --> 00:03:18,875
and spent his last years writing.

50
00:03:18,875 --> 00:03:21,716
He died in 1623, and none of his

51
00:03:21,796 --> 00:03:24,900
books was printed in his lifetime.

52
00:03:24,900 --> 00:03:28,286
The word shahbandar comes
from Persian and means,

53
00:03:28,366 --> 00:03:30,700
roughly, the lord of the harbour.

54
00:03:30,700 --> 00:03:33,104
In the Malay port kingdoms he was one

55
00:03:33,184 --> 00:03:35,600
of the great officials of the court.

56
00:03:35,600 --> 00:03:39,217
The 15th-century laws of
Melaka name four of them,

57
00:03:39,297 --> 00:03:44,250
each in charge of the foreign
merchants from one part of the world.

58
00:03:44,250 --> 00:03:48,554
The shahbandar looked after the
foreign merchants and their ships,

59
00:03:48,634 --> 00:03:51,225
including the dues they owed the ruler.

60
00:03:51,225 --> 00:03:54,696
After the Portuguese
captured Melaka in 1511,

61
00:03:54,776 --> 00:03:59,825
the sultan's family fled south
and set up the sultanate of Johor,

62
00:03:59,905 --> 00:04:02,825
with its capitals up the Johor River.

63
00:04:02,825 --> 00:04:05,739
Singapore became one of Johor's outposts,

64
00:04:05,819 --> 00:04:08,375
guarding the approach to the river.

65
00:04:08,375 --> 00:04:12,726
A shahbandar on the island means
that enough foreign ships still

66
00:04:12,806 --> 00:04:17,375
called there for the sultan to
keep a senior official on the spot.

67
00:04:17,375 --> 00:04:20,200
The Dutch records name him.

68
00:04:20,200 --> 00:04:24,116
In 1606 the Dutch admiral
Cornelis Matelief met the

69
00:04:24,196 --> 00:04:28,347
"Sabander van Sincapura",
the shahbandar of Singapore,

70
00:04:28,427 --> 00:04:33,050
who took him to meet Sultan
Alauddin Riayat Shah the Third.

71
00:04:33,050 --> 00:04:35,761
The meeting led to an alliance between

72
00:04:35,841 --> 00:04:39,000
Johor and the Dutch
against the Portuguese.

73
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:43,476
The Flemish gem trader Jacques de
Coutre, who spent the 1590s and

74
00:04:43,556 --> 00:04:49,364
early 1600s trading in these waters, called
Singapore "Ysla de la Sabandaria Vieja",

75
00:04:49,444 --> 00:04:52,448
the island of the old
shahbandar's quarters,

76
00:04:52,528 --> 00:04:57,575
and wrote that its port was one of
the best that served the East Indies.

77
00:04:57,575 --> 00:05:00,382
In the late 16th century the waters

78
00:05:00,462 --> 00:05:03,350
around Singapore became a war zone.

79
00:05:03,350 --> 00:05:07,916
In 1587 a Portuguese fleet
under Dom Paulo de Lima Pereira

80
00:05:07,996 --> 00:05:13,684
destroyed Johor Lama, the sultan's
fortified capital on the Johor River,

81
00:05:13,764 --> 00:05:17,850
and the court moved further
upstream to Batu Sawar.

82
00:05:17,850 --> 00:05:21,138
From the 1590s the Dutch
arrived to compete for

83
00:05:21,218 --> 00:05:24,649
the spice and China trade,
and Johor's rulers saw

84
00:05:24,729 --> 00:05:28,025
in them a counterweight
to Portuguese Malacca.

85
00:05:28,025 --> 00:05:31,737
In the months before the
trading season of 1603,

86
00:05:31,817 --> 00:05:37,900
the Portuguese commander André Furtado
de Mendonça blockaded the Johor River.

87
00:05:37,900 --> 00:05:41,929
On the 25th of February
1603, three Dutch ships under

88
00:05:42,009 --> 00:05:45,806
Admiral Jacob van Heemskerck
caught the Portuguese

89
00:05:45,886 --> 00:05:50,150
carrack Santa Catarina off
the east coast of Singapore.

90
00:05:50,150 --> 00:05:55,317
She was a ship of about 1,500 tons,
on her way from Macau to Malacca,

91
00:05:55,397 --> 00:05:58,511
and after a few hours of fighting her crew

92
00:05:58,591 --> 00:06:02,925
gave up the ship and her cargo
in return for their lives.

93
00:06:02,925 --> 00:06:07,053
The cargo included about 1,200
bales of Chinese raw silk and a

94
00:06:07,133 --> 00:06:11,125
great quantity of Ming porcelain,
and when the porcelain was

95
00:06:11,205 --> 00:06:15,469
sold in the Netherlands this kind
of blue-and-white became known

96
00:06:15,549 --> 00:06:19,825
there as "kraak" porcelain, after
the carracks that carried it.

97
00:06:19,825 --> 00:06:22,642
The sale raised the capital of the new

98
00:06:22,722 --> 00:06:26,000
Dutch East India Company
by more than half.

99
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:29,865
Not everyone in the Netherlands
approved of the seizure,

100
00:06:29,945 --> 00:06:34,525
and the Company asked a young
lawyer, Hugo Grotius, to defend it.

101
00:06:34,525 --> 00:06:39,345
His argument, part of which was
published in 1609 as Mare Liberum,

102
00:06:39,425 --> 00:06:42,685
"The Free Sea", held
that the oceans belonged

103
00:06:42,765 --> 00:06:46,700
to no one and were open to
the ships of every nation.

104
00:06:46,700 --> 00:06:50,569
It became one of the founding
texts of international law,

105
00:06:50,649 --> 00:06:53,975
and it began with a carrack
taken off Singapore.

106
00:06:53,975 --> 00:06:56,711
The map and the Dutch and Portuguese

107
00:06:56,791 --> 00:06:59,450
reports are not the only evidence.

108
00:06:59,450 --> 00:07:04,062
In 1998, before the Empress Place
Building on the north bank of

109
00:07:04,142 --> 00:07:08,382
the Singapore River became the
Asian Civilisations Museum,

110
00:07:08,462 --> 00:07:13,675
archaeologists led by John Miksic
dug on the riverbank in front of it.

111
00:07:13,675 --> 00:07:17,976
They found that the site had
been in use from the 14th through

112
00:07:18,056 --> 00:07:22,650
the 16th centuries, through the
time of Melaka and then of Johor.

113
00:07:22,650 --> 00:07:25,726
There was plenty of
Ming Chinese porcelain,

114
00:07:25,806 --> 00:07:32,477
including pieces from the 15th century, which
is rare outside China, and goods from Vietnam,

115
00:07:32,557 --> 00:07:36,300
Thailand, Java, Borneo,
the Philippines and Persia.

116
00:07:36,300 --> 00:07:40,346
In their report on the dig, Goh
Geok Yian and Miksic describe

117
00:07:40,426 --> 00:07:44,742
it as a place where goods from
much of maritime Asia were loaded,

118
00:07:44,822 --> 00:07:48,665
unloaded and passed on, and
suggest that the harbourmaster

119
00:07:48,745 --> 00:07:52,600
named on Erédia's map probably
lived on or near the site.

120
00:07:52,600 --> 00:07:56,100
The port did not survive
the wars for long.

121
00:07:56,100 --> 00:08:01,105
On the 4th of May 1613 a great
fleet sent by Sultan Iskandar

122
00:08:01,185 --> 00:08:06,525
Muda of Aceh, in northern Sumatra,
sailed into the Johor River.

123
00:08:06,525 --> 00:08:08,834
Batu Sawar fell on the 6th of June,

124
00:08:08,914 --> 00:08:13,543
and the Acehnese attacked the other
towns of the river and the coast,

125
00:08:13,623 --> 00:08:15,875
among them the town of Singapore.

126
00:08:15,875 --> 00:08:20,913
Borschberg writes that Singapura was
burned along with the upstream towns,

127
00:08:20,993 --> 00:08:24,175
perhaps because Johor's
fleet was based there.

128
00:08:24,175 --> 00:08:29,106
Many older histories tell it
differently, saying that the Portuguese

129
00:08:29,186 --> 00:08:34,050
burned the settlement at the mouth
of the Singapore River in 1613.

130
00:08:34,050 --> 00:08:36,407
Either way, the island's port does

131
00:08:36,487 --> 00:08:38,925
not recover in the written record.

132
00:08:38,925 --> 00:08:41,725
The name lingered for a while.

133
00:08:41,725 --> 00:08:45,936
A Portuguese chart of the
straits drawn in 1654 still

134
00:08:46,016 --> 00:08:50,550
marks a "Xebandaria" on the
island in one of its copies.

135
00:08:50,550 --> 00:08:54,180
After that the island drops
out of the records for most

136
00:08:54,260 --> 00:08:57,688
of two centuries, home to
communities of orang laut,

137
00:08:57,768 --> 00:09:01,950
the sea people, and passed by
the ships that used its straits.

138
00:09:01,950 --> 00:09:06,637
In 1811 about a hundred Malays
came over from Johor under the

139
00:09:06,717 --> 00:09:11,247
Temenggong and settled at the
mouth of the Singapore River,

140
00:09:11,327 --> 00:09:16,875
and that was the village Raffles
found when he arrived in January 1819.

141
00:09:16,875 --> 00:09:20,616
Why it matters today: Singapore's
story is often told as if

142
00:09:20,696 --> 00:09:24,567
Raffles found an empty island
in 1819, but Erédia's map shows

143
00:09:24,647 --> 00:09:29,489
that two centuries earlier it was still
a working port with a harbourmaster,

144
00:09:29,569 --> 00:09:33,116
a place where ships stopped,
paid their dues and traded,

145
00:09:33,196 --> 00:09:36,354
and where a quarrel between
European powers over a

146
00:09:36,434 --> 00:09:40,450
captured cargo helped give the
world the idea of the free sea.

147
00:09:40,450 --> 00:09:44,509
The job the shahbandar did
then is done today by the

148
00:09:44,589 --> 00:09:49,045
Maritime and Port Authority
of Singapore, set up in 1996,

149
00:09:49,125 --> 00:09:53,025
which runs one of the
busiest ports in the world.

150
00:09:53,025 --> 00:09:56,257
Two words on a small
Portuguese sketch are among

151
00:09:56,337 --> 00:09:59,925
the few traces left of the
port that came before it.
